00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:40.980
If I were to ask you to just do a little&nbsp;
sketch of what you think of when you hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:46.860
the phrase “moss animals,” you might imagine&nbsp;
something like this tardigrade—a waddling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:53.460
slightly fantastical creature that we did in fact&nbsp;
find living within the minuscule greenery of moss.&nbsp;

00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:00.180
But depending on who you are and what you know,&nbsp;
maybe you draw something completely different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:07.800
Maybe you draw something more like this. These&nbsp;
don’t really look much like an animal at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:13.440
do they? At first glance, they seem a bit&nbsp;
more like plants, with a stalk connecting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:19.980
series of flowers with thin, elegant petals.
But no, that is indeed an animal. One that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.120
has the dubious honor of being&nbsp;
defined largely by its anus.&nbsp;

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:32.760
Before we get there though, let’s start with&nbsp;
just explaining what these animals actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.940
are. These organisms are members of the phylum&nbsp;
Bryozoa, a name that translates in Greek to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:45.000
“moss animals”. But Bryozoa aren’t actually&nbsp;
animals that live in moss. They get their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.520
name because of what they look like when all of&nbsp;
the elegant individuals you see here congregate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:58.920
into colonies that look, ya know, mossy.
That giant gelatinous mass seems to bear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.920 --> 00:02:04.980
little resemblance to the tiny winsome creatures&nbsp;
that make it up. But Bryozoa are notable in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:11.880
animal kingdom because of just how colonial the&nbsp;
phyla is. Around the world, in waters salty and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:17.340
not, are colonies of different shapes and&nbsp;
sizes made up of individuals like these.&nbsp;

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.960
These colonies can get their start in&nbsp;
different ways. Some might be the result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:28.200
of sexual reproduction, sperm and egg that could&nbsp;
have met in the waters away from their makers. Or,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:35.100
in other cases, one colony may have captured sperm&nbsp;
released by another, using it to fertilize its own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:41.520
eggs, which stay brooding until a larva emerges,&nbsp;
ready to set out and develop its own colony.&nbsp;

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:48.360
But Bryozoa have another asexual option for&nbsp;
reproduction. Instead of fertilizing an egg,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:54.480
a colony can amass a bunch of cells into a&nbsp;
structure like this one, called a statoblast.&nbsp;

00:02:55.620 --> 00:03:01.560
These statoblasts can be so prevalent in our&nbsp;
samples that James, our master of microscopes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:07.200
has to scoop them out to keep them from being&nbsp;
the main focus of our videos. They are thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.120
and durable, able to lay dormant and protect&nbsp;
their contents from cold temperatures and dry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:17.940
weather. But when the conditions become right&nbsp;
again, they grow into a new individual that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:24.000
will form the basis of a new colony.
To do that, the individual Bryozoa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:29.580
called a zooid, navigates the waters as&nbsp;
a larva, eventually attaching itself to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:35.340
some kind of solid surface. Perhaps a piece&nbsp;
of rock, or some kind of shelf, maybe even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.920
some seaweed. And from there, it will begin to&nbsp;
divide, budding off but keeping its descendants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:48.300
close and connected as they increase in number.
Now, the arrangement of these zooids dictates the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:53.820
shape of their colony, as does the material&nbsp;
they secrete to support the colony overall.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.820 --> 00:04:01.140
Some excrete a gelatinous material that turns them&nbsp;
into those masses of jelly we saw earlier. Others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.780
rely on chitinous materials that give them more&nbsp;
rigidity. And as they spread over their surface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:14.040
and even grow up into the water around them,&nbsp;
Bryozoa colonies take on many spectacular shapes.&nbsp;

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:20.280
Even though Bryozoa are different from coral,&nbsp;
they definitely look similar. And like coral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:25.560
Bryozoa colonies don’t move. Instead&nbsp;
they remain attached to their surface.&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.020
But while they may not be able to move through&nbsp;
the world around them to gather resources, Bryozoa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.020 --> 00:04:37.140
are able to make the most of their surroundings,&nbsp;
with individual zooids taking on special functions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:43.800
that allow the group to accomplish more than&nbsp;
the individuals. Some focus on making eggs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:50.460
while others form small beaks that allow them&nbsp;
to snap at predators. And these zooids are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:56.520
dependent on another group of zooids that gather&nbsp;
food and share the nutrients with everyone else.&nbsp;

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:03.480
To get that food, the feeding zooids rely on&nbsp;
the lophophore, the ring of tentacles crowning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:09.660
every zooid’s bodies that looks a bit like a&nbsp;
bunch of thin petals or a very elegant duster.&nbsp;

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:15.060
Those tentacles are covered in cilia, which&nbsp;
circulate the waters around the Bryozoa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:20.280
stirring currents like the ones you see here&nbsp;
so that it can sweep bits of food like plankton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.920
towards the animal’s mouth.
And from certain angles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:28.920
you can see how the lophophore and the rest of&nbsp;
the zooid’s organs tuck into a sort of casing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:34.140
which is a structure called the cystid.
And while the Bryozoa has various other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.160
parts that we could talk about, what&nbsp;
we’re really here for is the anus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:43.860
which is a much bigger deal than you might&nbsp;
expect from an animal with such a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:50.640
digestive system. There’s a mouth, an esophagus,&nbsp;
and then a u-shaped gut that leads to the anus.&nbsp;

00:05:52.380 --> 00:05:58.620
The issue is really rooted in the existential&nbsp;
crisis at the heart of microcosmos: how are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.540
things related to each other? And what does&nbsp;
that mean for the names that we give to things?&nbsp;

00:06:06.780 --> 00:06:11.940
Organisms like these were originally&nbsp;
given the name Polyzoa in 1830,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:17.940
but that was changed a year later to Bryozoa&nbsp;
by the German naturalist Christian Ehrenberg.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:23.220
But as more organisms were added to the group,&nbsp;
the people watching them and describing them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:28.980
realized that they were actually lumping together&nbsp;
two very similar groups of organisms that actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.980 --> 00:06:33.180
had some key differences between them.
And one of those key differences was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:39.660
where the organism’s anus was located. One&nbsp;
group had their anus situated just outside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:44.760
their lophophore—that tentacle crown we were&nbsp;
watching gather food earlier. And one had their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:50.760
anus situated just inside their lophophore.
So to differentiate between the two groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.760 --> 00:06:57.540
scientists gave them very appropriate names. One&nbsp;
group was called Ectoprocta, which translates in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:03.780
Ancient Greek to “outside anus”. And the other&nbsp;
were called Entoprocta, which means “inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.780 --> 00:07:10.680
anus.” Because you can define anything by its&nbsp;
butt, as long as you turn to ancient languages.&nbsp;

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:16.140
In general, Bryozoa typically refers to the&nbsp;
Ectoprocts, though for many reasons (..I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:21.240
can’t possibly imagine why…), Bryozoa seems&nbsp;
to be the preferred name. But the history&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:27.540
and relationship between the Ectoprocts&nbsp;
and Entoprocts remains an ongoing mystery&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.540 --> 00:07:33.780
that scientists continue to explore.
They’re not the only creature out there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.780 --> 00:07:37.620
to be wrapped up in a phylogenetic&nbsp;
mystery. But isn’t it great to know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:44.040
especially for such a strange animal like&nbsp;
this, that the mystery is built on butts?
